Package: mos-board-x64
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mos build <mos@example.invalid>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: mos-system (= @VERSION@), mos-kernel-x64 (= @VERSION@), ${shlibs:Depends}
Description: mos board support -- x64 (QEMU/UEFI, A/B layout)
 Everything the image carries because it is the x64 board: /etc/fstab and the
 ESP mount unit rendered from boards/x64/board.env, the RAUC system
 configuration for its grub backend, and /usr/bin/grub-editenv. The ESP's own
 grub.cfg is not in here: the image assembler renders it from the repository,
 so a device copy would be an unrendered template nothing reads.
 .
 There is no vendor boot chain behind this board -- a UEFI machine's firmware
 provides it -- but the KERNEL is this repository's since PLAN-074, built by
 boards/x64/bsp and packaged as mos-kernel-x64, which this package depends on.
 It replaced Debian's linux-image-amd64 together with the entire initramfs
 apparatus: that kernel had no CONFIG_DM_INIT, so the dm-mod.create= verity
 table on the kernel command line was ignored here and an initrd had to
 re-implement it. mos-kernel-x64 carries the device mapper, dm-verity and
 squashfs built in, the board ships no initrd, and the dm-verity initramfs hook
 and local-top script this package used to install are gone with it.
 .
 initramfs-tools and cryptsetup-bin are no longer dependencies for the same
 reason. grub-editenv's libdevmapper is not named here: it is an ELF
 dependency, so ${shlibs:Depends} derives it, and with the version
 dpkg-shlibdeps reads.
